SH(1)                          User Commands                           SH(1)

NAME
    sh - the Horus shell

SYNOPSIS
    COMMAND [ARGUMENT]...
    COMMAND [ARGUMENT]... | COMMAND [ARGUMENT]... [| ...]

DESCRIPTION
    The shell asks for a login, then reads one line at a time and runs it.
    It is one ring-3 task that serves every login in turn: exit(1) clears
    the identity and returns to the login prompt.

    A line is split on spaces into words. There is no quoting, no
    variables and no globbing; a word is the characters between spaces.

    The first word names the command, found in this order:

      1. A line holding '|' is a pipeline of up to six programs from /bin,
         each one's output joined to the next one's input by a pipe.
      2. A bare name, with no '/', that is a file in /bin runs that
         program, with the other words as its arguments. A program in
         /bin takes precedence over a builtin of the same name, so the
         ported GNU cat, wc and echo replace the shell's own.
      3. Otherwise it is a builtin. `help` lists them and man(1) has a
         page for each.

    To run a program by path, use run(1).

    Every program the shell starts receives its filesystem from the shell,
    narrowed: a root and a working directory, never more than the shell
    holds, taken back when the program ends. It is given nothing else.

    The prompt is NAME@horus, then '#' for the administrator and '$' for
    anyone else.

FILES
    /bin            programs run by name
    /usr/share/man  the manual

SEE ALSO
    help(1), run(1), exit(1), hier(7), horus(7)
